On the other hand, even-order multipliers (e.g., frequency doublers) exploit push–push transistors, which inherently suppress the odd harmonics of the signal, including the fundamental component [24], [25]. The push–push topology consists of two differential MOS devices with connected drains at the output [as shown in Fig. 3(a)]. This concept can be generalized to generate the $N$th harmonic of the input signal by using N devices driven by signals that are phase-shifted by $2\pi /N$ relative to each other. This behavior can be understood by expressing the input signals as phase-shifted replicas of a common input. For an N-path structure, the inputs can be written as \begin{equation*} v_{k}\left ({ t}\right ) = V_{0} \cos \left ({ \omega _{o}t+\frac {2\pi k}{N}}\right ),\quad k=0,1,\ldots .,N-1. \tag {2}\end{equation*}
Fig. 3.
(a) Push–push doubler, (b) triple-push frequency multiplier, (c) differential implementation of the triple-push multiplier, and (d) vector representation of the harmonics generated by the N-push tripler.

The transfer functions corresponding to the 0° and 120° phases exhibit identical magnitude responses for all frequencies, as their numerators are complex conjugates while sharing the same denominator. This property ensures an inherent balance of amplitude between these two signals, independent of frequency. In contrast, the components associated with the 240° phase must be selected to ensure magnitude matching with the 0° and 120° phases at the center frequency. By equating the magnitudes of the transfer functions corresponding to the 0° and 240° phases at the design frequency ($\omega _{o1}$), a condition on the resonance frequencies of the two sections ($\omega _{o1}$, $\omega _{o2}$) can be derived as
\begin{align*} & \left \lvert { \frac {V_{out,240\pm }}{V_{in}}\left ({ \omega _{o1}}\right )}\right \rvert = \left \lvert { \frac {V_{out,0\pm }}{V_{in}}\left ({ \omega _{o1}}\right )}\right \rvert \tag {9}\\ & \sqrt {\frac {\left ({ 1+\beta ^{2}}\right )^{2}\omega _{o1}^{4}+\left ({ \frac {R_{2}}{L_{2}}\omega _{o1}}\right )^{2}}{\left ({ 1-\beta ^{2}}\right )^{2}\omega _{o1}^{4}+\left ({ \frac {R_{2}}{L_{2}}\omega _{o1}}\right )^{2}}}=\sqrt {1+\frac {4\omega _{o1}^{2}}{\left ({ R_{1}/L_{1}}\right )^{2}}} \tag {10}\end{align*}where $\omega _{o1}=1/\sqrt {(}L_{1}C_{1})$, $\omega _{o2}=1/\sqrt {(}L_{2}C_{2})$, and $\beta = \omega _{o2}/\omega _{o1}$. By simplifying the expressions and realizing the quality factors as: $Q_{1}=((L_{1}/C_{1}))^{1/2}/R_{1}$, and $Q_{2}=((L_{2}/C_{2}))^{1/2}/R_{2}$, the condition on the resonance frequency ratio ($\beta $) is
\begin{equation*} \frac {\left ({ 1+\beta ^{2}}\right )^{2}+\left ({ \frac {\beta }{Q_{2}}}\right )^{2}}{\left ({ 1-\beta ^{2}}\right )^{2}+\left ({ \frac {\beta }{Q_{2}}}\right )^{2}}=1+4Q_{1}^{2}. \tag {11}\end{equation*}

Using the following identity:
\begin{equation*} \tan \left ({ A-B}\right ) = \frac {\tan \left ({ A}\right )-\tan \left ({ B}\right )}{1+\tan \left ({ A}\right )\tan \left ({ B}\right )} \tag {20}\end{equation*}we get
\begin{align*} \frac {\sqrt {3}}{3} & = \frac {\frac {1}{2Q_{1}}-\frac {2\beta ^{3}Q_{2}}{\left ({ \beta ^{4}-1}\right )Q_{2}^{2}-\beta ^{2}}}{1+\frac {1}{2Q_{1}}\frac {2\beta ^{3}Q_{2}}{\left ({ \beta ^{4}-1}\right )Q_{2}^{2}-\beta ^{2}}} \tag {21}\\ \frac {\sqrt {3}}{3} & = \frac {\beta ^{2}Q_{2}^{2}-4\beta Q_{1}Q_{2}-1}{2Q_{1}\left ({ \beta ^{2}Q_{2}^{2}-1}\right )+2\beta Q_{2}}, \quad if {\,}\beta ^{4}\gt \gt 1. \tag {22}\end{align*}

Although both phase balance conditions cannot be achieved simultaneously at $\omega =\omega _{o1}$ with a finite $Q_{2}$, selecting a design frequency above $\omega _{o1}$ allows a feasible finite-Q solution. By re-visiting (9)





–(22), and solving for an arbitrary frequency ($\omega _{d}=x\omega _{o1}$, $x\gt 1$), the conditions required for phase and amplitude balances are
\begin{align*} Q_{1} & = \frac {\sqrt {3}x}{1+x^{2}} \tag {23}\\ \beta & = \sqrt {x^{4}+x^{2}+1} \tag {24}\\ Q_{2} & = \frac {\sqrt {3}x \sqrt {x^{4}+x^{2}+1}}{x^{4}-1}. \tag {25}\end{align*}
